In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

module: Fix kernel panic when a symbol st_shndx is out of bounds

The module loader doesn't check for bounds of the ELF section index in
simplify_symbols():

       for (i = 1; i < symsec->sh_size / sizeof(Elf_Sym); i++) {
		const char *name = info->strtab + sym[i].st_name;

		switch (sym[i].st_shndx) {
		case SHN_COMMON:

		[...]

		default:
			/* Divert to percpu allocation if a percpu var. */
			if (sym[i].st_shndx == info->index.pcpu)
				secbase = (unsigned long)mod_percpu(mod);
			else
  /** HERE --> **/		secbase = info->sechdrs[sym[i].st_shndx].sh_addr;
			sym[i].st_value += secbase;
			break;
		}
	}

A symbol with an out-of-bounds st_shndx value, for example 0xffff
(known as SHN_XINDEX or SHN_HIRESERVE), may cause a kernel panic:

  BUG: unable to handle page fault for address: ...
  RIP: 0010:simplify_symbols+0x2b2/0x480
  ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Fatal exception

This can happen when module ELF is legitimately using SHN_XINDEX or
when it is corrupted.

Add a bounds check in simplify_symbols() to validate that st_shndx is
within the valid range before using it.

This issue was discovered due to a bug in llvm-objcopy, see relevant
discussion for details [1].

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/linux-modules/20251224005752.201911-1-ihor.solodrai@linux.dev/

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

misc: bcm_vk: Corrección de posibles desreferencias de puntero nulo en bcm_vk_read()

En la función bcm_vk_read(), el puntero entry es verificado, indicando que puede ser NULL. Si entry es NULL y rc se establece en -EMSGSIZE, el siguiente código puede causar desreferencias de puntero nulo:

  struct vk_msg_blk tmp_msg = entry -> to_h_msg[0];
  set_msg_id(&tmp_msg, entry -> usr_msg_id);
  tmp_msg.size = entry -> to_h_blks - 1;

Para prevenir estas posibles desreferencias de puntero nulo, copie to_h_msg, usr_msg_id y to_h_blks de iter a variables temporales, y devuelva estas variables temporales a la aplicación en lugar de acceder a ellas a través de un entry potencialmente NULL.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

quota: Fix race of dquot_scan_active() with quota deactivation

dquot_scan_active() can race with quota deactivation in
quota_release_workfn() like:

  CPU0 (quota_release_workfn)         CPU1 (dquot_scan_active)
  ==============================      ==============================
  spin_lock(&dq_list_lock);
  list_replace_init(
    &releasing_dquots, &rls_head);
    /* dquot X on rls_head,
       dq_count == 0,
       DQ_ACTIVE_B still set */
  spin_unlock(&dq_list_lock);
  synchronize_srcu(&dquot_srcu);
                                      spin_lock(&dq_list_lock);
                                      list_for_each_entry(dquot,
                                          &inuse_list, dq_inuse) {
                                        /* finds dquot X */
                                        dquot_active(X) -> true
                                        atomic_inc(&X->dq_count);
                                      }
                                      spin_unlock(&dq_list_lock);
  spin_lock(&dq_list_lock);
  dquot = list_first_entry(&rls_head);
  WARN_ON_ONCE(atomic_read(&dquot->dq_count));

The problem is not only a cosmetic one as under memory pressure the
caller of dquot_scan_active() can end up working on freed dquot.

Fix the problem by making sure the dquot is removed from releasing list
when we acquire a reference to it.

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

sctp: mantener chunk -> transport sincronizado con la lista en la que está en cola

__sctp_outq_flush_rtx() mueve un chunk con acuse de recibo de brecha a la lista de transmitidos de otro transporte sin actualizar chunk -> transport:

	if (chunk -> tsn_gap_acked) {
		list_move_tail(&chunk -> transmitted_list,
			       &transport -> transmitted);
		continue;
	}

El chunk entonces permanece en la lista de un transporte activo mientras chunk -> transport todavía nombra a uno diferente. Si ese transporte es eliminado - sctp_assoc_rm_peer() de un ASCONF Delete-IP - sctp_transport_free() lo libera con RCU y el chunk queda con un puntero colgante. sctp_assoc_rm_peer() limpia peer -> transmitted y asoc -> outqueue.out_chunk_list, pero el chunk no está en ninguna de ellas.

El puntero no es seguido mientras tsn_gap_acked está establecido. Un SACK que revoca el TSN borra la bandera, y el siguiente SACK alcanza

	tchunk -> transport -> flight_size -= sctp_data_size(tchunk);

dentro del transporte liberado. KASAN reporta una lectura de uso después de liberación de slab en sctp_check_transmitted(), liberado desde sctp_assoc_rm_peer(). Tanto la eliminación como los SACK provienen del par de la asociación.

Establecer chunk -> transport en el movimiento. La ruta de reenvío ordinaria no necesita nada: alcanza su list_move_tail() solo después de que sctp_packet_append_chunk() haya devuelto SCTP_XMIT_OK, y __sctp_packet_append_chunk() ya haya reasociado el chunk para entonces.

Descubierto por XBOW, clasificado por Baul Lee <baul.lee@xbow.com>

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

sched/psi: Apagar rtpoll_timer en psi_cgroup_free()

psi_schedule_rtpoll_work() se llama sin bloqueo desde la ruta crítica del planificador y puede competir con psi_trigger_destroy() al eliminar el último disparador rtpoll bajo rtpoll_trigger_lock:

  psi_schedule_rtpoll_work()        psi_trigger_destroy()

  rcu_read_lock();
  task = rcu_dereference(rtpoll_task);
                                    rcu_assign_pointer(rtpoll_task, NULL);
                                    timer_delete(&rtpoll_timer);
  mod_timer(&rtpoll_timer, ...);
  rcu_read_unlock();
                                    synchronize_rcu();
                                    kthread_stop(task_to_destroy);

El grupo puede entonces ser liberado con el temporizador rearreglado aún pendiente, y poll_timer_fn() se ejecuta en memoria liberada.

461daba06bdc ('psi: eliminar kthread_worker del mecanismo de planificación de disparadores psi') eliminó el temporizador sincrónicamente después del synchronize_rcu(), lo que evitó esto pero en su lugar compitió con la creación del disparador: la eliminación podría cancelar el temporizador que un nuevo conjunto de disparadores armó durante el período de gracia y, como la creación también reinicializó el temporizador en ese momento, corromperlo.
8f91efd870ea ('psi: Corregir condición de carrera entre psi_trigger_create/destroy') movió la inicialización a group_init() y la eliminación a la sección bloqueada, intercambiando las condiciones de carrera de creación por la ventana anterior.

Ninguna de las ubicaciones en la ruta de destrucción funciona. Un temporizador pendiente que se dispara mientras el grupo está activo es inofensivo, sin embargo. poll_timer_fn() solo despierta la cola de espera rtpoll y no se rearma a sí mismo. Enlazar el temporizador a la vida útil del grupo en su lugar y apagarlo en psi_cgroup_free(). Nada puede armarlo para entonces. timer_shutdown_sync() porque el temporizador nunca se vuelve a armar.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

net/sched: act_ct: fix sk_buff leak when the header checks reject a packet

tcf_ct_handle_fragments() runs its header sanity checks before handing
anything to the defragmentation engine:

	if (family == NFPROTO_IPV4)
		err = tcf_ct_ipv4_is_fragment(skb, &frag);
	else
		err = tcf_ct_ipv6_is_fragment(skb, &frag);
	if (err || !frag)
		return err;

tcf_ct_ipv4_is_fragment() returns -EINVAL or -ENOMEM;
tcf_ct_ipv6_is_fragment() adds -EPROTO when ipv6_find_hdr() fails. None of
them frees or queues the skb, so on that path the caller still owns it.

tcf_ct_act() however funnels every non-zero return into the
ownership-transfer exit:

	err = tcf_ct_handle_fragments(net, skb, family, p->zone, &defrag);
	if (err)
		goto out_frag;
	...
out_frag:
	if (err != -EINPROGRESS)
		tcf_action_inc_drop_qstats(&c->common);
	return TC_ACT_CONSUMED;

TC_ACT_CONSUMED means the action took ownership of the skb, so no caller
frees it - sch_handle_ingress(), sch_handle_egress() and
tcf_qevent_handle() all deliberately skip the free for that verdict. The
skb is therefore orphaned: one sk_buff plus its data buffer is leaked per
malformed packet, unbounded. Note the drop counter is already incremented
for these errors, so the statistics claim a drop that never happens.

Three different ownership states reach out_frag: today - the skb may be
queued by the defrag engine (-EINPROGRESS), already freed by
nf_ct_handle_fragments(), or still owned by us. Tell the caller which of
those it is, and free the packet ourselves in the last case, which
restores the TC_ACT_SHOT behaviour that predated the Fixes: commit.

Reproduced on v7.2-rc6 with a 54-byte frame carrying a 40-byte IPv6
header with nexthdr = 0 (hop-by-hop) and nothing after it, on a
clsact ingress chain with "action ct". kmemleak reports one leaked
232-byte skbuff_head_cache object plus its 704-byte data buffer per
packet; with this patch it reports none.

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

net/sched: act_ct: corrige la fuga de sk_buff cuando las comprobaciones de cabecera rechazan un paquete

tcf_ct_handle_fragments() ejecuta sus comprobaciones de integridad de cabecera antes de entregar cualquier cosa al motor de desfragmentación:

	if (family == NFPROTO_IPV4)
		err = tcf_ct_ipv4_is_fragment(skb, &frag);
	else
		err = tcf_ct_ipv6_is_fragment(skb, &frag);
	if (err || !frag)
		return err;

tcf_ct_ipv4_is_fragment() devuelve -EINVAL o -ENOMEM;
tcf_ct_ipv6_is_fragment() añade -EPROTO cuando ipv6_find_hdr() falla. Ninguno de ellos libera o encola el skb, por lo que en esa ruta el llamador todavía lo posee.

tcf_ct_act() sin embargo canaliza cada retorno no nulo hacia la salida de transferencia de propiedad:

	err = tcf_ct_handle_fragments(net, skb, family, p -> zone, &defrag);
	if (err)
		goto out_frag;
	...
out_frag:
	if (err != -EINPROGRESS)
		tcf_action_inc_drop_qstats(&c -> common);
	return TC_ACT_CONSUMED;

TC_ACT_CONSUMED significa que la acción tomó posesión del skb, por lo que ningún llamador lo libera - sch_handle_ingress(), sch_handle_egress() y tcf_qevent_handle() omiten deliberadamente la liberación para ese veredicto. El skb queda, por lo tanto, huérfano: un sk_buff más su búfer de datos se fuga por cada paquete malformado, sin límites. Nótese que el contador de caídas ya se incrementa para estos errores, por lo que las estadísticas afirman una caída que nunca ocurre.

Tres estados de propiedad diferentes alcanzan out_frag: hoy - el skb puede ser encolado por el motor de desfragmentación (-EINPROGRESS), ya liberado por nf_ct_handle_fragments(), o todavía en nuestra posesión. Informar al llamador cuál de ellos es, y liberar el paquete nosotros mismos en el último caso, lo que restaura el comportamiento TC_ACT_SHOT que precedió al commit de Fixes:.

Reproducido en v7.2-rc6 con una trama de 54 bytes que transporta una cabecera IPv6 de 40 bytes con nexthdr = 0 (salto a salto) y nada después de ella, en una cadena de entrada clsact con 'action ct'. kmemleak informa de un objeto skbuff_head_cache de 232 bytes fugado más su búfer de datos de 704 bytes por paquete; con este parche no informa de ninguno.

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

ALSA: usb-audio: corrige escritura fuera de límites en URBs entrantes de Tipo II

data_ep_set_params() dimensiona cada búfer de transferencia URB antes de añadir el delimitador de transferencia de Formato Tipo II:

	u -> packets = urb_packs;
	u -> buffer_size = maxsize * u -> packets;

	if (fmt -> fmt_type == UAC_FORMAT_TYPE_II)
		u -> packets++; /* for transfer delimiter */
	u -> urb = usb_alloc_urb(u -> packets, GFP_KERNEL);

buffer_size se calcula a partir del recuento de paquetes previo al incremento y nunca se vuelve a calcular, por lo que para un punto final de Tipo II el búfer tiene un paquete menos que el recuento de paquetes con el que se construye el URB.

prepare_inbound_urb() luego organiza un marco iso por paquete y nunca consulta buffer_size:

	offs = 0;
	for (i = 0; i < urb_ctx -> packets; i++) {
		urb -> iso_frame_desc[i].offset = offs;
		urb -> iso_frame_desc[i].length = ep -> curpacksize;
		offs += ep -> curpacksize;
	}

	urb -> transfer_buffer_length = offs;
	urb -> number_of_packets = urb_ctx -> packets;

El último descriptor, por lo tanto, apunta un paquete más allá del final del búfer de transferencia, donde el controlador de host escribe datos del dispositivo en cada transferencia entrante. prepare_silent_urb() y prepare_playback_urb() limitan sus bucles de llenado por ctx -> buffer_size, por lo que solo la captura se ve afectada.

fmt_type proviene de los descriptores de transmisión de audio del dispositivo, por lo que cualquier dispositivo que anuncie un formato de captura de Tipo II encuentra esto una vez que el espacio de usuario establece hw_params en el flujo.

KASAN en 7.2.0-rc5 (arm64) con un dispositivo dummy_hcd/raw-gadget, un informe por transferencia entrante:

  BUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in dummy_timer
  Write of size 64 at addr ffff0000186171c0 by task cons02/166
   __asan_memcpy
   dummy_timer
   hrtimer_run_softirq
  Allocated by task 166:
   usb_alloc_coherent
   snd_usb_endpoint_set_params
  The buggy address is located 0 bytes to the right of
   allocated 64-byte region [ffff000018617180, ffff0000186171c0)

Calcule buffer_size después de que se haya contabilizado el paquete delimitador, y limite el bucle de llenado por buffer_size, como ya hace prepare_silent_urb() en el lado de salida. Esto aumenta cada asignación de URB de Tipo II en un paquete de tamaño máximo.

Descubierto por XBOW, clasificado por Baul Lee <baul.lee@xbow.com>

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

hwmon: (ltc4282) Limitar los límites de corriente negativos

Cuando se pasa un valor negativo a ltc4282_write_curr(), el 'signed long' 'val' se convierte directamente a 'u64':

drivers/hwmon/ltc4282.c:ltc4282_write_curr() {
        /* es necesario pasarlo en milivoltios */
        u32 in = DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST_ULL((u64)val * st -> rsense, DECA * MICRO);
        ...
}

Esta conversión convierte las entradas negativas en valores positivos grandes. El resultado de la división subsiguiente desborda la variable 'u32 in', truncándose a un valor positivo pseudoaleatorio. Cuando esto se pasa a ltc4282_write_voltage_byte(), se limita al límite máximo en lugar de cero.

Limitar 'val' a 0 y al límite superior máximo soportado antes de la conversión y asignar el resultado a una variable temporal de 64 bits antes de la división para evitar el subdesbordamiento y un posible desbordamiento también.

En el kernel de Linux, la siguiente vulnerabilidad ha sido resuelta:

hwmon: (pmbus) Corrige la confusión de tipos en la lógica de notificación

Sashiko informa:

Al inicio del bucle en pmbus_notify(), el código convierte incondicionalmente cada atributo a una struct sensor_device_attribute:

drivers/hwmon/pmbus/pmbus_core.c:pmbus_notify() {
    for (i = 0; i < data -> num_attributes; i++) {
        struct device_attribute da = to_dev_attr(data -> group.attrs[i]);
        struct sensor_device_attribute attr = to_sensor_dev_attr(da);
        int index = attr -> index;
...
}

Sin embargo, data -> group.attrs puede contener otros tipos como struct pmbus_samples_reg o struct pmbus_sensor, que solo incrustan una struct device_attribute base.

Si da es una struct pmbus_samples_reg, dev_attr es el último miembro. Convertirlo a struct sensor_device_attribute y leer el campo index parece acceder a memoria más allá del final de la asignación, lo que podría desencadenar una lectura fuera de límites de slab.

Además, si da es una struct pmbus_sensor, convertirlo causa que el campo index se superponga con los campos page, phase y reg. ¿Podría esto producir una máscara de basura en sistemas little-endian que coincida espuriamente con los reg, page y flags objetivo durante una alerta?

Soluciona el problema usando struct sensor_device_attr en struct pmbus_sensor y struct pmbus_label. Dado que esos atributos nunca desencadenan una notificación, establece el valor de attr -> index en -1 para ellos. Usa este valor para distinguir de los atributos booleanos que _sí pueden_ desencadenar una notificación y usan el campo index para codificar los valores de máscara, página y registro.

In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:

drop_monitor: fix out-of-bounds write in reset_per_cpu_data()

In reset_per_cpu_data(), al is computed as:

    al = sizeof(struct net_dm_alert_msg);
    al += dm_hit_limit * sizeof(struct net_dm_drop_point);
    al += sizeof(struct nlattr);

    skb = genlmsg_new(al, GFP_KERNEL);
    ...
    nla = nla_reserve(skb, NLA_UNSPEC, sizeof(struct net_dm_alert_msg));
    ...
    msg = nla_data(nla);
    memset(msg, 0, al);

Because al includes sizeof(struct nlattr) (the 4-byte attribute header),
genlmsg_new() allocates al bytes of tailroom starting at nla.
However, msg points to nla_data(nla), which is located
sizeof(struct nlattr) bytes past nla. Calling memset(msg, 0, al)
therefore writes al bytes starting from msg, exceeding the allocated
buffer by sizeof(struct nlattr) (4 bytes) and corrupting
skb_shared_info.

Fix this by letting al represent only the payload length, allocating
the skb with genlmsg_new(nla_total_size(al), GFP_KERNEL), and zeroing
al bytes from msg.

A Use After Free vulnerability in the packet forwarding engine (pfe) of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows an unauthenticated, network-based attacker to cause a Denial-of-Service (DoS).



When web filtering for TCP traffic is configured, a high volume of DNS requests increases the likelihood of a flowd crash and restart, which leads to a complete service outage until the service has automatically recovered. This is a race condition outside the attacker's direct control.

This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S5;
  *  from 23.4 before 23.4R2-S6;
  *  from 24.2 before 24.2R2-S3;
  *  from 24.4 before 24.4R2-S1;
  *  from 25.2 before 25.2R1-S1, 25.2R2.

An OS command injection vulnerability in the CLI of Juniper Networks Junos OS allows a local, low privileged attacker to take complete control of the device.

A specific 'request csds' command doesn't sanitize user provided input so that it can be interpreted as a command. This allows unintended command execution with root privileges, which can lead to unauthorized system access or modification.





This issue affects Junos OS:


  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S4;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R2-S1;
  *  25.4 versions before 25.4R1-S3;






This issue does not affect Junos OS releases before 24.4R1.

An Improper Resource Locking vulnerability in the clock synchronization daemon (clksyncd) of Juniper Networks Junos OS on MX Series allows a unauthenticated, logically adjacent attacker to cause a Denial-of-Service (DoS).



If a SyncE or PTP upstream node sends a malformed message to an MX device equipped with SCBE3, a false 19.44MHz alarm is generated on the master RE. This leads to persistent RE switchovers and a clksyncd process crash. This issue is outside the attacker's direct control as it's a race condition.

This issue affects Junos OS on MX Series:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S7.7;
  *  23.4 versions before 23.4R2-S8.13;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S5.5;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S4;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R2-S1;
  *  25.4 versions before 25.4R1-S2.

A Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime vulnerability in the packet forwarding engine (PFE) of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows an unauthenticated, network-based attacker to cause a complete Denial-of-Service (DoS).



On SRX4000 Series, and SRX5000 Series with SPC3, and MX Series with SPC3, when one or more specific application services like ALGs, IDP/DPI, APPID, SECINTEL, AAMW or AppQoE are configured, a high connections per second churn will cause PFE memory to leak. If the memory is exhausted traffic processing will be impacted.

Use the CLI command show security monitoring to monitor the memory utilization of the PFEs on the FPCs.

This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series and MX Series:


  *  23.4 versions from 23.4R2-S3 before 23.4R2-S7;
  *  24.2 versions from 24.2R2 before 24.2R2-S3;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S3;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R1-S2, 25.2R2.

An Improper Handling of Missing Special Element vulnerability in the SSL plugin of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows a network-based, unauthenticated attacker to cause a Denial-of-Service (DoS).



This issue is will occur when 'ssl-proxy' is configured on SRX platforms and during a TLS session-resumption attempt a specifically malformed TLS message is received. This results in an invalid pointer access and PFE crash which causes a temporary loss of traffic forwarding until the PFE automatically recovers. The exact internal timing to trigger this issue is outside the attacker's direct control.This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S5;
  *  23.4 versions before 23.4R2-S5;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S2;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2.

An Improper Resource Locking vulnerability in the UTM processing of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows an unauthenticated, network-based attacker to cause a Denial-of-Service (DoS).



When UTM WebFiltering is configured and the WebFiltering cloud server is unreachable, sustained HTTP traffic load may cause the UTM subsystem to crash and restart, which will lead to a complete service outage until the system has recovered.
When category lookup requests cannot be completed, they accumulate internally. Under high load and specific timing conditions, an invalid internal state can occur during cleanup of pending WebFiltering lookups, resulting in a crash of the UTM process.
This issue applies to both NextGeneration WebFiltering (NGWF) and Enhanced WebFiltering (EWF).

This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series:


  *  23.2 versions from 23.2R2 before 23.2R2-S7.7;
  *  23.4 versions from 23.4R2 before 23.4R2-S8.13;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S5.5;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S4;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R2-S1;
  *  25.4 versions before 25.4R1-S3, 25.4R2.

An Improper Validation of Syntactic Correctness of Input vulnerability in the VPN daemon of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows an authenticated, network-based attacker to take complete control of the device.

If an SRX device configured for AutoDiscovery VPN (ADVPN) receives a message from a peer that contains one or more specifically malformed attributes, this will cause a buffer overflow, which can allow remote code execution and therefore allow an attacker to take complete control of the device. To be able to carry out this attack an attacker must either have acquired the shared secret to impersonate a peer or have compromised an existing one, so this attack is considerably difficult.

This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S7.7;
  *  from 23.4 before 23.4R2-S7;
  *  from 24.2 before 24.2R2-S4;
  *  from 24.4 before 24.4R2-S3;
  *  from 25.2 before 25.2R2;
  *  from 25.4 before 25.4R1-S3.

An Improper Restriction of Communication Channel to Intended Endpoints vulnerability in Juniper Networks Junos OS Evolved allows an unauthenticated, network-based attacker to cause a limited information disclosure and availability impact to the device.

Due to a wrong initialization, a process which should only be able to communicate internally within the device can be reached over the network via an open port. This leads to a device being inadvertently exposed and increased CPU cycles spent processing ingress packets.
This issue affects Junos OS Evolved:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S7.7-EVO;
  *  23.4 versions before 23.4R2-S8.11-EVO;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S5.6-EVO;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S4-EVO;
  *  25.2 version before 25.2R2-S1-EVO;
  *  25.4 versions before 25.4R1-S1-EVO.





Please note that this issue is similar to but not identical CVE-2026-33803.

A NULL Pointer Dereference vulnerability in the DNS plugin of Juniper Networks Junos OS on SRX Series allows an unauthenticated, network-based attacker to cause a complete Denial-of-Service (DoS).



When multiple DNS queries are received in a single flow session, where one query matches a Security Intelligence (SecIntel) feed and another query is sent for DNS Domain Generation Algorithm (DGA) analysis, an error in processing logging of these asynchronous tasks can lead to an FPC crash. The exact timing conditions are outside an attacker's direct control.

This issue affects Junos OS on SRX Series:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S7.7;
  *  from 23.4 before 23.4R2-S8.13;
  *  from 24.2 before 24.2R2-S5.5;
  *  from 24.4 before 24.4R2-S4;
  *  from 25.2 before 25.2R2-S1;
  *  from 25.4 before 25.4R1-S2.

An Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs vulnerability in the routing protocol daemon (rpd) of Juniper Networks Junos OS and Junos OS Evolved allows an unauthenticated, adjacent attacker to cause a Denial-of-Service (DoS).

When in a BGP traffic engineering scenario a specific, valid, optional, non-transitive BGP update is received over an established BGP session, RPD crashes and automatically restarts, which leads to traffic impact until routing has reconverged.

This issue affects:
Junos OS:


  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S6;
  *  23.4 versions before 23.4R2-S7;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S4;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S4;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R1-S2, 25.2R2;



Junos OS Evolved:
  *  All versions before 23.2R2-S6-EVO;
  *  23.4 versions before 23.4R2-S8.11-EVO;
  *  24.2 versions before 24.2R2-S4-EVO;
  *  24.4 versions before 24.4R2-S5-EVO;
  *  25.2 versions before 25.2R1-S2-EVO, 25.2R2-EVO;

A Stack-based Buffer Overflow vulnerability in the kmd component of Juniper Networks Junos OS on MX Series allows an unauthenticated network attacker to execute code as root via specific IKE certificate-based packets.

This issue affects Junos OS on MX Series:


  *  All versions before 21.2R3-S11;
  *  from 21.4 before 21.4R3-S12;
  *  from 22.2 before 22.2R3-S8;
  *  from 22.3 before 22.3R3-S5;
  *  from 22.4 before 22.4R3-S9;
